#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Duty Board v3.124.0 — HR 4: Leave Management (pass 4 of 8).

The entitlement managed, not the favour negotiated: leave types and
the Nigerian statutory floor, policies and allocation, the leave
application, the balance ledger, holiday lists, leave planning
against coverage, and the special situations. TEN chapters (the
track's deep module, like Accounts 5), 35-bank.

Deploy: apply -> commit -> then on the server:
  bench --site xlevel.clouderp.one execute duty_board.academy_seed_hr_pro.seed_hr_pro_track

Anchored, idempotent. Requires v3.123.0.
Run from ~/frappe-bench/apps/duty_board.
"""

import io
import json
import os
import sys

INIT = "duty_board/__init__.py"
DATA_PATH = "duty_board/academy_hr_pro_data.json"
SEEDER_PATH = "duty_board/academy_seed_hr_pro.py"
CHECK_ONLY = "--check" in sys.argv

L = lambda t, est, html: {"title": t, "est": est, "html": html}
Q = lambda q, opts, ans, why, src: {"q": q, "opts": opts, "ans": ans, "why": why, "src": src}

LESSONS = [
L("Chapter 1 — Leave: the entitlement managed, not the favour negotiated", "12", "<p>Module 3's taxonomy had one category too big for a paragraph: approved leave. This module gives it its machinery — and opens with the thesis that reframes how most trading firms think about it: <b>leave is an entitlement managed, not a favour negotiated.</b></p><p><b>The two diseases.</b> Unstructured leave fails in two opposite directions, and the consultant meets both in the same week: <b>leave as favour</b> — the request granted by mood and standing, where the supervisor's friends holiday and the quiet workers don't ask, where nobody knows their balance because there IS no balance, only negotiations — the register's politics (module 3) applied to rest, corroding fairness exactly where the workforce watches most closely; and <b>leave unmanaged</b> — the opposite failure, where leave is technically granted but structurally untaken: no planning, no encouragement, no tracking, and the firm quietly accrues both a financial LIABILITY (untaken leave is money owed — Chapter 5's ledger makes it visible) and a human one (the exhausted workforce, and the never-absent employee Chapter 10 will name as one of the trade's classic fraud tells). The cure for both is the same machinery: entitlements defined by policy, balances kept as a ledger, applications as documents riding the spine, and planning that meets the roster — the module's map.</p><p><b>The entitlement frame.</b> The reframe the consultant teaches owners, because it changes every downstream conversation: leave is not a gift the firm dispenses; it is part of the EMPLOYMENT BARGAIN — earned by service, defined by policy sitting on a statutory floor (Chapter 2), and owed as surely as salary. The firm's discretion lives in WHERE policy sets the offer above the floor and WHEN leave is taken (the scheduling conversation — Chapter 7's coverage tension is real and legitimate), never in WHETHER the entitlement exists. The frame pays immediately: the manager who understands leave as owed schedules it; the one who understands it as a favour hoards it — and hoarded leave becomes December's collapse (Chapter 7) and the balance-sheet liability the accountant will eventually price (Chapter 5).</p><p><b>The coverage tension, named honestly.</b> The trading firm's legitimate anxiety, stated at the door so the module can answer it: the branch must trade (module 3's roster law), and leave removes rostered capacity — so the entitlement and the coverage pull against each other, and the resolution is neither denial (the favour disease) nor chaos (everyone gone in December) but PLANNING: leave spread deliberately across the year, visible on the calendar managers roster from, approved with the coverage check, and protected by the blackout rules the trade's peaks genuinely justify (Chapter 7). A firm that plans leave trades every day AND rests its people — the tension is managed, not won — and the module's machinery exists precisely to make that management routine instead of heroic.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Leave types & the Nigerian statutory floor", "14", "<p>The taxonomy, and the layer of law beneath it. The consultant's altitude here mirrors the tax module's: know the FLOOR exists and roughly where it sits, configure the policy the firm sets ABOVE it, and route the law's reading to the employment adviser who owns it.</p><p><b>The statutory floor, at working altitude.</b> Nigerian employment law sets minimums the firm's policy cannot undercut for the workers the law covers: <b>annual leave</b> — the Labour Act's entitlement after twelve months of continuous service (the statutory minimum for ordinary workers is famously modest — six working days — which is precisely why Chapter's second half matters: market practice offers well above it, and the floor is a floor, not a benchmark); <b>sick leave</b> — the Act's provision for paid sick absence (up to twelve working days a year, on the conditions the Act sets, medical certification among them); <b>maternity leave</b> — the protected leave the Act guarantees (twelve weeks, with the pay provision the Act attaches and the dismissal protections around it — the area where employer error is most expensive and most scrutinised); and the surrounding rules (public holidays as paid days, the timing and pay-in-lieu constraints) the adviser confirms. The consultant's discipline, verbatim from the tax module: these numbers are the ORIENTATION, not the advice — the Act's coverage definitions, its exceptions, and the reform landscape are the employment adviser's terrain, and the policy page records what the adviser confirmed, dated, reviewed annually.</p><p><b>The firm's types.</b> The working set a Nigerian trading firm configures: <b>annual leave</b> (the core entitlement, set at the firm's policy level — commonly well above the floor, graded by seniority where the firm chooses), <b>sick leave</b> (with its certification rules — Chapter 4), <b>maternity leave</b> (the statutory shape, run properly — Chapter 8), <b>casual leave</b> (the short-notice personal-matters allowance many firms offer — small, bounded, and distinct from annual so the annual entitlement stays plannable), <b>compassionate leave</b> (bereavement — defined, so the worst weeks of people's lives are not also negotiations), <b>examination/study leave</b> (where the firm supports development — module 6's terrain meeting this one), and <b>leave without pay</b> (the governed exception — Chapter 8). Each type a configured record: its entitlement, its accrual, its notice rules, its documentation requirements, its pay treatment flagged for the boundary.</p><p><b>Floor versus policy: the design conversation.</b> The chapter's central distinction, taught with the owner in the room: the LAW sets what the firm must do; the POLICY sets what the firm chooses to do — and the choice is a labour-market position, not a compliance question. The firm competing for good storekeepers and accountants against the market's practice offers the market's leave or explains itself at every offer stage; the firm resting on the statutory six days is technically compliant and commercially noticeable. The policy page records the choices per type and grade, the adviser's confirmation of the floor beneath them, and the review date — and the configuration then ENFORCES the policy evenly (the system granting what the page says, to everyone the page covers), which is the entitlement frame made mechanical: the offer is the offer, for everyone, visibly.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Policies & allocation: granting the entitlement", "13", "<p>The policy becomes days in a balance through ALLOCATION — the machinery that grants entitlements per person per period, prorated at the edges, governed at the carry-over. The granting chapter.</p><p><b>The policy attaches by structure.</b> Module 1's architecture pays off again: leave policies attach by GRADE and type (the grade's annual entitlement — the band structure carrying its first benefit), by employment TYPE (the full-timer's entitlement versus the part-timer's proportion versus the contractor's contract terms — module 1's type field driving policy exactly as promised), and by tenure rules where the policy sets them (the entitlement that steps up at year three). The attachment discipline: policy lives in the CONFIGURATION, not in per-person edits — the individually adjusted entitlement is the favour disease wearing an admin screen, and exceptions above policy are policy CHANGES (versioned, dated, applied to the class) or documented individual terms (the contract that genuinely differs), never quiet numbers.</p><p><b>Allocation mechanics.</b> The grant itself, per period: the ANNUAL allocation (the year's entitlement granted at the period's start — simple, plannable, the small firm's default) versus ACCRUAL (the entitlement earned monthly as service accrues — the method that matches the earning to the year and simplifies the leaver's arithmetic, at the cost of early-year balances that constrain planning; the choice is policy, made once, applied evenly). PRORATION at the edges: the joiner's first year granted proportionally from their start (the pipeline's date-of-joining field — module 1's anchor doing this exact work), the leaver's final year prorated to their exit (module 7's full-and-final computation consuming this number across the boundary). And the allocation as a RECORD: each grant a document (person, type, period, days, basis) — because the balance Chapter 5 builds is a ledger, and ledgers are built from documents, not adjustments.</p><p><b>Carry-forward: the governed remainder.</b> The year ends with balances unspent, and the policy must say what happens — the three clean answers: LAPSE (use-it-or-lose-it — maximally simple, maximally pressuring toward Chapter 7's December problem), CARRY with a CAP (the market's common middle — a bounded number of days crossing the year, the cap keeping the liability from compounding), or ENCASH (the payout for untaken days — a policy decision with real cost, computed across the payroll boundary, and governed so it doesn't become a standing salary top-up that pays people NOT to rest: Chapter 7 treats encashment properly). The governance conviction: whatever the rule, it is WRITTEN, applied evenly, and executed by the year-end allocation ritual (the carry-forward computed and granted as documents, the lapses logged, the encashments listed for the boundary) — the year boundary crossed by machinery, not by a January of arguments about what December's balances meant.</p><p><b>The allocation calendar.</b> The rhythm this chapter adds to module 8's page: the annual allocation run (the new year's grants, the carry-forward execution — scheduled, owned, verified), the joiner's allocation inside the onboarding checklist (module 2's day-one readiness including the leave balance that self-service will show), and the periodic accrual run where that method rules. Allocation done this way is invisible; done by memory it is January's dispute season — the module's quiet economics.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — The leave application: the document & the spine", "13", "<p>The entitlement is spent through a DOCUMENT — the leave application — and the document's flow is everything module 1 and 3 built, working together. The spending chapter.</p><p><b>The application's anatomy.</b> The employee raises it (self-service — the join of module 1 paying its daily dividend): the type, the dates, the reason where the type asks for one, and the system's own contributions at the moment of application — the BALANCE CHECK (the application against an insufficient balance is caught at birth, not discovered in payroll: the days available shown as the form fills), the HOLIDAY overlay (the applied span netted of holidays and off-roster days per the policy's counting rules — working days versus calendar days, decided once in configuration), and the half-day option where policy offers it. The application lands in the approver's queue along the SPINE (module 1's reports-to field routing it to the person who owns the coverage consequence), and the approval or decline lands back with its reason — the whole conversation a document trail, which is the point: the leave that was 'agreed in the corridor' and never recorded is module 3's absence-mark dispute waiting to happen.</p><p><b>Notice: applied in advance, per type.</b> The policy's notice rules, enforced by the flow: annual leave applied ahead by the stated horizon (the fortnight-for-a-week norm many firms use — enough for the roster to adjust; Chapter 7's planning makes long leave visible far earlier), casual leave's shorter fuse (the type exists precisely for the short-notice reality — bounded so it cannot become the annual entitlement's queue-jumping route), and the approval SLA on the firm's side (the application that sits unanswered for a week teaches people to revert to corridors — the spine's approver owns a response time, and module 8's read watches it). The evenness conviction, again: notice rules bind everyone, and the senior whose leave appears retroactively teaches the policy's real name.</p><p><b>Sick leave: the retroactive reality.</b> The one type whose document follows the absence, governed accordingly: the sick absence reported on the day (the contact protocol — module 3's no-show machinery distinguishing the reported-sick from the unexplained absence from hour one), the application regularised on return within the policy's window, and the CERTIFICATION rule applied as written (the medical certificate for absences past the policy's threshold — commonly beyond two days, per the firm's policy on the statutory frame; required evenly, filed to the record). The design balance the consultant states plainly: certification thresholds exist because sick leave without any evidence rule becomes the elastic extension of every weekend, AND the rule is set humanely because demanding paperwork for every single day taxes the genuinely ill and the clinic queues — the policy's number is a trust calibration, made once, applied evenly, reviewed against Chapter 10's pattern reads rather than adjusted per suspicion.</p><p><b>Cancellations and changes.</b> The lifecycle's edges: the approved leave cancelled before it starts (the document reversed, the balance restored, the roster released — by flow, not deletion), the leave cut short by recall (the genuine operational emergency — governed: the recall is a management decision with a name on it, the untaken days restored, and the pattern read, because the firm that habitually recalls has a planning disease wearing an urgency costume), and the extension mid-leave (a fresh application against the balance, decided on coverage like any other). Every edge a document; the balance ledger of Chapter 5 always reconciling to the trail.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Balances & the leave ledger", "13", "<p>Behind every application sits the number that governs it: the BALANCE — and this chapter builds it properly, as a ledger, because that is what it is. The accounting chapter of the people track.</p><p><b>The ledger grammar, applied to days.</b> The balance is not a field someone edits; it is a RUNNING LEDGER per person per leave type: allocations CREDIT it (Chapter 3's documents), approved applications DEBIT it (Chapter 4's), cancellations reverse, carry-forwards credit the new year, encashments debit against their payout, and the balance at any moment is the ledger's sum — never an adjusted number, always a derived one. Every conviction from the money tracks transfers verbatim: the balance that doesn't reconcile to its documents is a broken ledger (find the undocumented movement — it exists); adjustments are exceptions with reasons (the correction document, visible forever); and history is frozen (last year's ledger is sealed by the year-end ritual — the January argument about December's balance is answered by pointing, not remembering). The consultant who has taught four tracks of ledgers teaches this one in ten minutes, and the accountant in the room smiles: it is the same grammar, denominated in days.</p><p><b>Visibility: the self-service dividend.</b> The balance SHOWN — each employee seeing their own, per type, with the ledger behind it (the allocations and takings listed — the transparency that answers <i>why is my balance 11?</i> with a trail instead of a negotiation) — is module 1's biggest single query-killer: the balance question, asked of HR in every unstructured firm weekly, simply stops existing. Managers see their TEAM's balances (the spine defining visibility as always — the coverage planner needs to know who is carrying 18 days into Q4: Chapter 7's planning read starts here), and HR sees the population (the distribution read: the balances clustering high are Chapter 7's planning failure ripening; the negative outliers are governance leaks).</p><p><b>Negative balances: the governed edge.</b> The policy question the configuration must answer: may leave be taken beyond the balance? The clean default is NO (the balance check refusing at application — the entitlement spent as earned), with the governed exception where policy allows it (the advance against the next allocation — bounded in days, approved above the spine's first level, and recorded as the negative ledger position it is, because an ungoverned negative balance is a loan nobody priced: the leaver with minus-eight days is a full-and-final deduction conversation module 7 will have to host, and the policy should have had it first).</p><p><b>The liability view: the accountant's interest.</b> The chapter's boundary-crossing insight, taught because it converts owners: untaken leave is not just a wellbeing datum — it is a FINANCIAL LIABILITY (days owed that will be paid, as leave or as encashment or in the leaver's final settlement), and the accrued-leave provision is a balance-sheet line the accountant computes from THIS ledger (Accounts 4's provision discipline, fed by HR's data — the boundary handoff's quiet second passenger). The read that follows: the leave liability trended (the firm whose balances compound yearly is deferring a cost and calling it flexibility), per branch and grade (where the hoarding lives), and priced (the accountant's valuation making the wellbeing conversation a naira conversation — the language owners hear fastest). The ledger built this chapter serves three masters at once — the employee's clarity, the manager's planning, the accountant's provision — which is what a master data layer is FOR, and why the notebook it replaces (Chapter 10's pattern) failed all three.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Holiday lists & the calendar", "12", "<p>The year's shared days off — the holiday list — small machinery with outsized reach, because every attendance expectation and leave computation reads it. The calendar chapter.</p><p><b>The holiday list as a record.</b> Per year, per applicable population: the list of public holidays (the Nigerian federal set — the two Eids, Christmas and Easter's days, Independence Day, New Year, Workers' Day, Democracy Day — plus the state-declared days where branches sit in states that observe them: the branch-specific list is real machinery for the multi-state firm, and module 1's branch field is what it attaches by), created ANNUALLY as a ritual (the year's list drafted from the known calendar, adopted, published — module 8's calendar carries the task) and AMENDED as declarations land — because the Nigerian reality the consultant plans for is that holidays are sometimes declared or moved days in advance (the lunar-calendar holidays confirmed on sighting; the government's occasional additions), and the amendment flow (the list updated, the affected rosters adjusted, the workforce notified through the standing channel) is the difference between a calendar quirk and a payroll dispute.</p><p><b>What the list wires into.</b> The reach, itemised: ATTENDANCE expectations (module 3's taxonomy — the holiday is not an absence; the marked day resolves against the list automatically), LEAVE computation (Chapter 4's netting — the leave span crossing a holiday doesn't spend the holiday, per the policy's counting rule), the ROSTER (the trading firm's honest complication: retail often TRADES on public holidays — the branch open on a holiday staffs by the holiday-working policy the firm sets, with the premium or lieu-day treatment the policy defines and the payroll boundary computes; the roster for a trading holiday is planned like a Saturday, and the fairness machinery of module 3's rotation covers who works it), and the PAYROLL boundary (holiday pay treatments crossing with the attendance package — the categories carrying the treatment, as always).</p><p><b>The optional-holiday pattern.</b> Where firms observe it — the restricted or optional holiday (the day granted to those who observe it, worked by those who don't, commonly around religious observances the workforce splits across): configured as its own small type or list flag, governed evenly (the observance taken at the employee's declaration, not the supervisor's assessment), and rostered with the same coverage logic as everything else. The point is not the pattern's universality — many firms simply use the federal list — but the consultant's readiness: the multi-faith workforce is the Nigerian trading floor's normal condition, and the firm that handles observance by policy handles it without friction; the one that handles it by negotiation rehearses the favour disease at its most sensitive point.</p><p><b>The calendar as shared surface.</b> The closing frame: the holiday list, the leave calendar (Chapter 7), and the roster (module 3) are three views of ONE planning surface — the year as the firm will actually work it — and the implementation that publishes them together (the branch's wall and phone showing the same truth) has made the working year legible to everyone who lives inside it. Legibility is the quiet product: the workforce that can SEE the year plans against it, and half of Chapter 7's discipline happens by itself.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Leave planning, coverage & encashment", "14", "<p>The module's operational summit: leave meets the roster, and the tension Chapter 1 named gets its machinery. Planning, the coverage check, the blackout rules, and the encashment governance.</p><p><b>The leave calendar.</b> The manager's planning surface: approved and pending leave visible across the team and the months (the spine defining the view), read TOGETHER with the roster (module 3's coverage law consuming this exact data — the approved leave blocks the slot BEFORE the roster is cut), and consulted at approval (the coverage check as part of the decision, not an afterthought: the application whose dates leave Saturday uncovered comes back with the honest conversation — <i>these dates strand the branch; these alternatives don't</i> — which is the entitlement honoured AND the branch trading: the tension managed in one screen).</p><p><b>Spreading the year: the planning conversation.</b> The proactive half — because approval-time checks manage requests, and planning PREVENTS the crunch: the annual leave-planning conversation (early in the year, per team: entitlements on the table, preferences gathered, the year sketched — the December-heavy trade's staff steered toward the January-October window with the honesty that December is the trade's harvest), the quarterly balance read (Chapter 5's manager view — the person carrying 18 days into Q4 gets the nudge in September, not the collision in December), and the use-it culture the firm deliberately builds: leave ENCOURAGED, modelled by seniors (the manager who never rests teaches the team that rest is weakness — and hoards the liability), and protected (the approved leave that gets cancelled by habitual 'emergencies' teaches people not to plan, which re-manufactures the December rush the planning existed to prevent).</p><p><b>Blackouts: the peak protected, honestly.</b> The trading calendar's legitimate claim: the windows where the trade cannot spare capacity (the December retail peak, the season's harvest weeks, the stock-count dates of Inventory 4 — the events the year is built around), declared as BLACKOUT or restricted windows in policy — announced at the year's start (not sprung in November), applied evenly (the blackout that binds the floor but not the office is the favour disease with a calendar), bounded (the blackout is weeks, not quarters — the restriction that swallows a third of the year is a staffing-level problem wearing a policy), and COMPENSATED in spirit (the December worker gets January's first pick — the rotation fairness of module 3, applied to the year). Emergency leave inside a blackout remains what it always is — the compassionate and sick realities don't read calendars — and the policy says so, so the blackout constrains planning, never humanity.</p><p><b>Encashment: the governed payout.</b> The policy question with money attached: paying for untaken days. The governance the consultant configures: encashment as a BOUNDED exception (the cap on encashable days — commonly the carry-forward's excess or a stated maximum — not an open market where the workforce sells its rest back annually), PRICED across the boundary (the computation is payroll's — the rate basis the accountant owns; HR's ledger supplies the days), and READ for what it reveals (the encashment list is the planning-failure report wearing a payout: the same names yearly means those roles cannot release their people, which is a coverage-design finding, not a payments routine). The design conviction stated for owners who like encashment's simplicity: systematically paying people not to rest optimises the liability and abandons the wellbeing — the entitlement's point was the rest; the payout is the exception that keeps the ledger honest at the edges, and the firm that finds itself encashing broadly should fix its planning, not enlarge its budget.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Special situations: the governed edges", "14", "<p>The leave layer's hard cases — the situations where governance matters most because stakes, law, and humanity converge. The edges chapter, at consultant altitude with the adviser's boundary bright.</p><p><b>Maternity leave, run properly.</b> The statutory shape (twelve weeks with the Act's pay provision and its protections — the adviser confirms the current reading and any state variations), and the PROCESS the firm wraps around it, which is where implementations differ: the notification handled with the confidentiality module 1's custody frame demands, the leave planned EARLY (the known start window makes cover planning a months-ahead exercise, not a scramble — the cover plan documented: who holds the till authority, the warehouse custody, the approval roles during the absence, which is the academy's handover discipline applied to a planned absence), the return handled deliberately (the return date confirmed, the role held as the law requires, the re-entry supported — the returning employee finding their access intact and their desk expected is module 2's day-one readiness, second edition), and the record trail complete throughout — because this is the leave category where employer missteps carry the heaviest legal and reputational weight, and process is protection for everyone.</p><p><b>Extended sick leave.</b> Beyond the certification threshold into the long absence: the escalating documentation the policy defines (the certificates' cadence, the medical review at the stated milestone), the pay treatment steps the policy sets on the statutory frame (full, then the policy's reduced provisions — the adviser's terrain, the configuration's enforcement), the contact discipline (the welfare check-ins that are support, scheduled and logged — the long-absent employee who never hears from the firm learns what they were worth), and the eventual capability question where recovery stalls (the process the employment framework governs strictly — module 7's terrain, never improvised here beyond the note that it EXISTS and is a process, not a deletion).</p><p><b>Leave without pay.</b> The governed exception: granted by policy's criteria (the exhausted balance with genuine need; the extended personal circumstance), approved above the spine's first level (the unpaid month is a bigger decision than a leave day), bounded in duration, and crossed to the boundary correctly (the unpaid days in the attendance package's categories — the treatment payroll applies is exactly why the taxonomy exists). The governance point: LWOP as a safety valve is humane; LWOP as a routine channel is a symptom (the entitlement too small, the planning failed, or the attendance machinery leaking — the read tells which).</p><p><b>Study leave & the development bond.</b> Where module 6's development machinery meets this one: the study leave policy (what the firm supports, at what pay, with what evidence of enrolment and progress), and the BOND concept at consultant altitude — the service commitment some firms attach to sponsored study (the agreement the adviser drafts and the firm applies evenly; the consultant's job is the record: the bond's terms filed, its dates tracked by module 1's expiry machinery, its status visible at separation when module 7's full-and-final asks).</p><p><b>The boundary distinctions.</b> The edges where leave meets other machinery, kept crisp: leave during NOTICE (module 7's rules — commonly constrained by policy so notice periods serve their purpose; the balance's fate resolved in the final settlement), leave versus ABANDONMENT (module 1's ghost revisited with its process — the unexplained extended absence walked through the contact protocol and the documented abandonment procedure, never a quiet status flip), and SUSPENSION versus leave (module 7's disciplinary machinery is not a leave type — the distinction that keeps both records honest). Every edge, a document; every document, the adviser-confirmed process behind it — the chapter's whole method in one line.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Case study: HealthTrade's leave layer", "16", "<p>The entitlement machinery, installed. Every chapter clocks in.</p><p><b>Policy and allocation (Ch. 2-3).</b> The design sessions with the employment adviser and the MD: annual leave set well above the floor (graded — 15 working days at grade 1 rising to 21 at grade 3; the market-position conversation held explicitly: <i>we hire against firms offering this</i>), sick leave on the statutory frame with the two-day certification threshold, casual leave at four bounded days, compassionate defined, and the policy page signed and dated with the adviser's floor confirmation attached. Allocation runs annual-grant with joiner proration; carry-forward capped at five days; the year-end ritual scheduled. The allocations run as documents — 27 grants, each a record — and self-service shows every balance by week's end: the balance-question emails stop within the month, as promised.</p><p><b>The flows, live (Ch. 4-5).</b> The applications ride the spine with the balance check at birth; the first decline is instructive — a storekeeper's dates stranding a delivery-day Tuesday, returned with alternatives, re-applied and approved in a day (the tension managed, visibly fairly). A negative-balance request (a grade-1 assistant, three days short, family obligation) is handled by the written advance rule: approved one level up, bounded, recorded as the ledger position it is. The sick-leave certification rule meets its first test evenly: a four-day absence certificated and filed — the same rule, the following month, for a supervisor: the evenness noticed, as it always is.</p><p><b>The calendar and the peak (Ch. 6-7).</b> The holiday list adopted in January; the Eid confirmation lands three days ahead and the amendment flow runs cleanly (list updated, two rosters adjusted, the announcement through the standing channel — a calendar quirk, not a dispute). The December blackout is declared in FEBRUARY with its honest framing (<i>December is our harvest; January's first pick goes to December's workers</i>) — and the leave-planning conversations spread the year visibly: the Q3 balance read catches two heavy carriers, nudged in September, resting in October. December trades fully staffed for the first time in the firm's memory; January's first-pick list honours the promise, publicly.</p><p><b>The maternity case (Ch. 8).</b> Mid-year, the Ibadan senior till operator: notification handled with the custody discipline, the twelve weeks planned four months ahead — the cover plan documented (till authority delegated to the named second, the Saturday roster re-rotated, the approval role remapped for the duration), the adviser's pay-provision reading applied through the boundary, check-ins scheduled and logged, and the return prepared (access intact, the role held, the first week's roster eased). The debrief line in the file: <i>the process protected everyone — including the firm.</i></p><p><b>The liability read (Ch. 5, 7).</b> Year-end, the boundary's quiet second passenger: HR's ledger hands the accountant the accrued-leave position; the provision computes at ₦1.9m — trended DOWN from the pre-system estimate (the planning spread having converted hoarded days into taken rest), with the encashment list at three names, each explained (the cap's excess, priced and paid through payroll). The MD's year-note joins the collection: <i>we used to negotiate rest; now we plan it — and it costs less.</i> The leave layer ends its first year with balances that reconcile, a December that traded, and the entitlement visibly the same for everyone — which was the thesis, delivered.</p>"),
L("Chapter 10 — Common mistakes & the fourth law", "13", "<p>The leave layer's scar tissue. Symptom → disease → fix.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — leave as favour.</b> Symptom: the supervisor's friends holiday; the quiet workers don't ask; no balances, only negotiations. Disease: the entitlement dispensed by mood (Ch. 1) — the register's politics applied to rest. Fix: policy by grade and type, allocation as documents, applications on the spine, and the evenness that binds seniors first — the offer is the offer, for everyone, visibly.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the December collapse.</b> Symptom: half the branch applying for the peak; approvals by seniority scramble; the branch trading short in its harvest month. Disease: no planning, no blackout, no year-spreading (Ch. 7). Fix: the blackout declared in February with its compensating first-pick, the annual planning conversation, the quarterly balance nudges — the year spread by machinery.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — balances in a notebook.</b> Symptom: HR's notebook and the employee's memory disagreeing; January's argument about December's days; the leaver's balance negotiated at exit. Disease: the number without the ledger (Ch. 5). Fix: the ledger grammar — allocations credit, applications debit, the balance always derived, the year sealed by ritual — and self-service showing the trail that ends the argument.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the never-taken leave.</b> Symptom: balances compounding yearly; the indispensable person who never rests; the liability growing quietly. Disease: three at once (Ch. 1, 5, 7) — the wellbeing failure (burnout wearing dedication), the financial failure (the deferred cost the accountant will price), and the CONTROL failure the money tracks taught this academy to name: the person who never takes leave is one of fraud's classic tells, because schemes that need daily tending cannot survive their operator's absence — which is why mature control environments make leave MANDATORY for sensitive roles, and why the reconciler who hasn't rested in three years should worry a consultant more than the one who has. Fix: the use-it culture, the balance reads, and — for the custody and finance roles four tracks protected — the deliberate absence as a control, not a courtesy.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — certification by suspicion.</b> Symptom: sick-leave evidence demanded from some and waived for others; the rule flexing by standing. Disease: trust calibrated per person instead of per policy (Ch. 4). Fix: the threshold written, applied evenly, reviewed against pattern reads — the policy doing the trusting so no supervisor has to.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — holiday chaos.</b> Symptom: the declared holiday that surprises payroll; branches guessing whether they open; the Eid roster improvised at midnight. Disease: the list without its amendment flow (Ch. 6). Fix: the annual list ritual, the amendment protocol (update, re-roster, announce), and the holiday-working policy set before the year needs it.</p><p><b>The fourth law.</b> The record, the pipeline, the capture — and now the entitlement: <b>leave is an entitlement managed, not a favour negotiated — defined by policy on the statutory floor, granted as documents, spent through applications on the spine, kept as a ledger that always reconciles, planned against the trading year, and governed evenly at every edge from maternity to the last unspent day.</b> The balance serves the employee's clarity, the manager's coverage, and the accountant's provision at once; the December that trades and the workforce that rests stop being rivals. Module 5 turns from the year's rhythm to the person's growth: performance — the conversation with a record.</p>"),
]

